"In the first five verses of Surah an-Naziat, an oath is sworn by the entities that perform five acts: pulling out violently, drawing out gently, swimming/gliding along, racing ahead, and arranging affairs. The response (jawab) to this oath is not explicitly stated because it is understood from the subsequent verses, meaning: 'The Day of Judgment and the resurrection after death will certainly come to pass.'\n\nBecause words such as \"nazi'at\", \"gharq\", \"nashit\", and \"nasht\" in these verses carry multiple meanings, it is not possible to translate them with a single meaning. Therefore, it is necessary to seek to understand them through tafsir (exegesis).\n\nThe word \"gharq\" carries meanings such as plunging into water, filling a vessel, and drawing a bow vigorously. As for \"nazi'at\", it is derived from the root \"naz'\" and possesses meanings such as death throes, drawing a bucket from a well, bending a bow, and galloping horses. It also carries the meaning of yearning for something by deriving from the root \"nuzu'\". Accordingly, nazi'at may be the angels that take souls, fighting forces, or souls that yearn for their bodies.\n\n\"Nashitat\", on the other hand, comes from the root \"nasht\", which means gently pulling or untying a knot. For this reason, nashitat has been interpreted as the angels who take the souls of the believers with ease, or the planets moving from one constellation to another. If it is derived from \"nashat\", it means joy and cheer of the heart, in which case it indicates that the souls of the believers depart in peace.\n\nCommentators have offered different interpretations regarding the entities mentioned in these verses: First, all of them are angels; angels of punishment who violently extract the souls of the disbelievers, angels of mercy who gently take the souls of the believers, and angels who traverse by divine command and manage affairs. According to the second interpretation, they are human souls; virtuous souls departing from the body or perfected human beings engaged in spiritual struggle against the lower self (nafs). The third interpretation is that they are warriors (ghazis) or their horses. The fourth is the stars, though acting as managers of affairs is not fitting for them.\n\nThe clearest interpretation is that these are angels, virtuous souls, or forces. By swearing upon these entities, it is emphasized that the Day of Judgment and the resurrection will surely occur. The manner in which the verbs in the verses are connected is also noteworthy; the first three verbs are connected with \"waw\" (and), whereas the last two verbs are connected with \"fa\" (then/so), indicating that the final two actions are the result of the preceding ones.\n\nThe words appearing at the beginning of Surah an-Naziat, such as nazi'at, gharq, nashitat, and nasht, have multiple lexical meanings, so it is impossible to translate them with a single meaning. For this reason, one must endeavor to understand them through tafsir. While gharq means plunging into water to submerge/drown, or drawing a bow fully; nazi'at can derive from the roots 'naz'' (to pull, pluck, tear out) or 'nuzu'' (to incline toward, yearn).\n\nThe word nazi'at has been interpreted in various ways, such as angels violently snatching souls, dying souls in their death throes, forces extracting souls by pulling from the depths, warriors drawing their bows, or galloping horses. In addition, likened to an exile yearning for their homeland, the meaning of souls separated from their bodies has also been given to it. As for nashitat, it stems from the roots 'nasht' (to pull gently, to untie a knot) or 'nashat' (joy of the heart); it has been interpreted as the angels of mercy who gently extract the souls of the believers or as the souls departing from the body with joyful contentment.\n\nCommentators have transmitted four main interpretations regarding these words: First, they are all angels; nazi'at are the angels of punishment, nashitat are the angels of mercy, sabihat are the angels traversing by divine command, sabiqat are the angels conveying souls to Paradise or Hell, and mudabbirat are the angels managing affairs by Allah's command. The second interpretation is that they are human souls; the virtuous souls of the deceased or souls progressing along the spiritual path. The third interpretation is that they are warriors or their horses. Although the fourth interpretation is the stars, the attribute of 'arranging/managing affairs' is not suitable for them; the most accurate view is that they are angels or virtuous souls.\n\nThe response to the oath made with these five actions (pulling violently, drawing gently, gliding along, racing ahead, and arranging affairs) has been concealed; however, it is emphasized that the Day of Judgment and the resurrection after death will definitely take place. Then, the terror of the Resurrection is described: 'The quaking commotion (al-Rajifah) and the second commotion that follows it (al-Radifah).' Al-Rajifah has been interpreted as the first blast (of the Trumpet), and al-Radifah as the second blast.\n\nThe deniers, saying, 'Shall we indeed be returned to our former state? Even after we have become decayed bones?', deem resurrection impossible and mock it. In response, Allah states, 'It will be but a single cry, and behold, they will be awakened in the open plain.' Here, as a lesson regarding the end of the deniers, the narrative transitions to the story of Moses (Musa); recounting Pharaoh's claim, 'I am your supreme lord,' and Allah commanding Moses to ask, 'Do you have the will to purify yourself?' This narrative was elaborated in earlier surahs and is presented here in a concise and brief manner.\n\nAllah the Almighty asks, rebuking the disbelievers: Is it harder to create you, or to create the heaven? He built the heaven, raised its canopy, and proportioned it. He darkened its night and brought forth its morning brightness. This demonstrates the greatness of His power.\n\nAfter that, He spread out and leveled the earth. From it He drew forth its water and its pasture, and firmly set the mountains. All of this is for the provision of you and your livestock. Creation is not in vain; it possesses a wisdom and a purpose.\n\nBut when the great, overwhelming calamity (the Resurrection) arrives, man will realize what he was striving for. On that day, Hell will be made plainly visible for all who see. Everyone will see it and witness its terrifying state.\n\nThen, as for whoever transgressed and preferred the life of this world, indeed, Hell will be his refuge. That person did not obey Allah's commands while in this world, but followed the desires of his lower self.\n\nAnd as for whoever feared standing before his Lord and restrained his soul from vile desires, indeed, Paradise will be his refuge. This person walked the straight path in the world with concern for the Hereafter.\n\nThey ask when the Hour will arrive. How could you know of it? The knowledge of it belongs to your Lord alone. You are only a warner for those who fear it. On the day they see it, it will seem to them as if they had not tarried in this world except for an evening or a morning thereof."